    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "the torque converter solenoid sounds like a good place to start. but i'm also seeing some debris in the pan when i drop it. could those pressure spikes be caused by something physical getting stuck in the valve body, or is it more likely an electronic issue with the TCM like you mentioned?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That debris in the pan is a massive clue, and honestly, it's much more likely to be a physical obstruction than a purely electronic glitch, though the two often work together in a transmission like this. When you see metal shavings or sludge, it usually means the internal gears or bearings have been grinding against each other, creating a physical mess that can easily jam the valve body solenoids or the pressure control valves.\n\nThink of the valve body like a complex maze of tiny tunnels; if a piece of debris gets stuck in one of those tunnels, it physically blocks the hydraulic fluid from flowing smoothly, causing those erratic pressure spikes you're seeing. It's a mechanical blockage, not just a software error. However, the TCM might be trying to compensate for this blockage by sending weird signals, which is why your scan tool is showing such irregular data.\n\nYou should definitely inspect the valve body carefully for any stuck valves or debris before replacing the solenoids, because swapping a new solenoid on top of a blocked valve body is like trying to fix a clogged pipe by replacing the faucet—it won't solve the root problem. If the debris is heavy, you might even need to replace the entire valve body assembly",
      "source": "target",
